      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: DIAGNOSTIC TESTS
Description: Diagnostic tests are procedures and tools used to diagnose or evaluate diseases, including cancer. In the context of oncology, they are used to confirm the presence of cancer, determine whether the disease is localized or advanced, and help plan appropriate treatment. These tests also play a critical role in monitoring patients after treatment, as they can detect whether cancer is still growing or spreading. Diagnostic tests include a range of methods such as imaging scans (e.g., CT, MRI, or X-rays), biopsies (removal and examination of tissue samples), and genetic or biomarker tests that analyze DNA, genes, proteins, or other molecular markers. In addition, some diagnostic procedures, such as lumbar puncture, may be used to evaluate diseases affecting the nervous system or to check for the spread of cancer into the cerebrospinal fluid. By providing detailed information about the presence, type, and extent of disease, diagnostic tests are essential for accurate diagnosis, staging, treatment planning, and ongoing surveillance to assess the effectiveness of therapy and detect any recurrence or progression.
Relationships:
  - Source: DIAGNOSTIC TESTS
    Target: BIOPSY
    Description: Diagnostic tests include biopsies
  - Source: BIOPSY
    Target: DIAGNOSTIC TESTS
    Description: A biopsy is one type of diagnostic test used to confirm prostate cancer
  - Source: DIAGNOSTIC TESTS
    Target: TREATMENT
    Description: Diagnostic tests help plan treatment after a diagnosis and can monitor whether cancer is growing or spreading after treatment
  - Source: DIAGNOSTIC TESTS
    Target: IMAGING TESTS
    Description: Diagnostic tests include imaging scans, biopsies, and genetic tests; imaging tests are a type of diagnostic test
  - Source: DIAGNOSTIC TESTS
