    <node id="GENETIC COUNSELOR">
      <data key="d0">GENETIC COUNSELOR</data>
      <data key="d1">GENETIC COUNSELOR</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">A genetic counselor is a health professional with specialized training in genetic diseases and cancer genetics. They help patients understand gene changes related to disease and hereditary cancer risk, and assist individuals in deciding whether to undergo germline testing. In addition, genetic counselors interpret the results of genetic tests, providing expert guidance and counseling. They may also provide counseling as recommended by the National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) for people with a genetic risk of skin cancer. Overall, genetic counselors support patients in making informed decisions about genetic testing and understanding the implications of their genetic test results.
Relationships:
  - Source: GENETIC TESTING
    Target: GENETIC COUNSELOR
    Description: Genetic testing may be done through a genetic counselor, who helps patients understand the results
  - Source: GENETIC COUNSELOR
    Target: GENETIC TESTING
    Description: A genetic counselor helps interpret the results of genetic tests
  - Source: GENETIC TEST
    Target: GENETIC COUNSELOR
    Description: A genetic counselor helps patients decide on and interpret genetic tests.

---</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GENETIC TEST">
      <data key="d0">GENETIC TEST</data>
      <data key="d1">GENETIC TEST</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">A genetic test is a medical test used to identify abnormal changes, known as mutations, in genes. These changes can indicate an inherited risk for certain cancers, such as inherited cancer risk. In the context of prostate cancer care, genetic testing is divided into two basic types: germline testing and molecular biomarker testing. Germline testing looks for inherited gene mutations, while molecular biomarker testing analyzes the tumor itself to detect genetic alterations that can guide treatment decisions. Together, these approaches help assess cancer risk, prognosis, and potential targeted therapies.
Relationships:
  - Source: GENETIC TEST
    Target: MUTATION
    Description: A genetic test is used to find abnormal changes (mutations) in genes.
  - Source: GENETIC TEST
    Target: GERMLINE MUTATION
    Description: Genetic testing can detect germline mutations relevant to cancer risk.
  - Source: GENETIC TEST
    Target: CARE TEAM
    Description: The care team puts together all test results, including genetic tests, to determine the level of prostate cancer risk.
  - Source: GENETIC TEST
    Target: PROSTATE CANCER
    Description: A genetic test finds abnormal gene changes associated with inherited risk for cancer
  - Source: DOCTOR
    Target: GENETIC TEST
    Description: The doctor can talk to the patient about genetic testing.
  - Source: GENETIC TEST
    Target: GERMLINE TESTING
    Description: GENETIC TEST is a broad category of medical analysis used to identify changes in chromosomes, genes, or proteins. GERMLINE TESTING is a specific form of GENETIC TEST that focuses on inherited cancer risk, meaning it looks for genetic mutations present from birth that may predispose an individual to certain cancers. In the context of prostate cancer care, GERMLINE TESTING is recognized as one of the two basic types of genetic tests used, alongside somatic testing (which examines tumor tissue). As such, GERMLINE TESTING serves both to assess hereditary cancer susceptibility and to guide treatment decisions for prostate cancer, making it a key tool in precision oncology.
  - Source: GENETIC TEST
    Target: GENETIC COUNSELOR
    Description: A genetic counselor helps patients decide on and interpret genetic tests.
  - Source: GENETIC TEST
    Target: MOLECULAR BIOMARKER TESTING
    Description: Molecular biomarker testing is one of the two basic types of genetic tests used for prostate cancer care.
  - Source: GENETIC TEST
    Target: SOMATIC MUTATION
    Description: Genetic testing can detect somatic mutations that arise later in life.
  - Source: GENETIC TEST
    Target: MEDICAL PROVIDERS
    Description: Medical providers can discuss and order genetic testing.

---</data>
    </node>
