    <node id="SURGICAL STAGING">
      <data key="d0">SURGICAL STAGING</data>
      <data key="d1">SURGICAL STAGING</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">SURGICAL STAGING, also known as pathologic staging, is a procedure performed during surgery to determine the extent of cancer, most notably ovarian cancer. It is considered the most accurate method for staging ovarian cancer. During the procedure, a surgeon removes and tests lymph nodes and takes tissue samples from organs and areas where the cancer may have spread. Specifically, para-aortic lymph nodes are typically removed, while pelvic lymph nodes may or may not be removed depending on the case. This process allows a pathologist to assess tumor growth and lymph node involvement, ultimately assigning a cancer stage of I, II, or III. While the procedure is often minimally invasive, it is fundamentally a surgical intervention that provides critical information for treatment planning and prognosis.

**Relationships:** No relationships listed for this entity in the retrieved data.

---</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PATHOLOGIC STAGING">
      <data key="d0">PATHOLOGIC STAGING</data>
      <data key="d1">PATHOLOGIC STAGING</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Pathologic staging, also referred to as surgical staging, is the post-surgical process of determining the extent to which cancer has spread. It is based on laboratory findings from tissue and lymph nodes removed during surgery. The stage is assigned by a pathologist who examines the surgically removed specimens, such as a lymph node, to rate the cancer. This staging method is also known as the surgical stage and is designated with categories such as pN (pathologic node stage). Pathologic staging provides more precise information about the cancer than clinical staging because it is derived from direct examination of actual tissue. It is commonly used for various cancers, including ovarian cancer, where the post-surgery determination of how far the disease has spread relies on information gained during surgery and surgical staging.

**Relationships:**
  - Source: PATHOLOGIC STAGING
  - Target: CANCER STAGING
  - Description: Cancer staging is the overall process of determining the extent of cancer in the body. It is often performed as pathologic staging after surgery. Pathologic staging is the post-surgery portion of cancer staging, determined from surgical tissue.

---</data>
    </node>
