    <node id="COLECTOMY">
      <data key="d0">COLECTOMY</data>
      <data key="d1">COLECTOMY</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">COLECTOMY is a surgical procedure involving the removal of part or all of the colon. It is the primary surgical treatment for non-metastatic colon cancer, performed to excise the cancerous portion of the colon along with nearby lymph nodes, which are then tested to determine the extent of disease. This surgery is typically done before decisions about adjuvant chemotherapy are made. The procedure can be performed in several ways: as a one-stage operation where healthy colon ends are reconnected; with a colostomy, where the bowel is diverted to an opening in the abdominal wall; or after stenting to unblock the bowel. Potential side effects and complications include anastomotic leaks, changes in bowel habits, bowel obstruction due to scar tissue, and hernia formation.
Relationships: None in the provided relationship data.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
