      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Abdominoperineal resection (APR) is a radical surgical procedure performed through a transabdominal approach. It is used to treat persistent, progressive, or recurrent anal cancer, as well as tumors in the lower rectum that may have grown into the anus or pelvic floor muscle. The procedure involves the removal of the anus, the rectum, the area where the rectum and colon meet (rectosigmoid junction), and surrounding fat. Additional tissues may also be removed, including levator muscles and, in some cases, groin lymph nodes. APR always requires a permanent colostomy, as the natural passage for waste elimination is removed.
