    <node id="IN SUMMARY, SURGERY AND CHEMOTHERAPY ARE BOTH ESSENTIAL AND COMPLEMENTARY TOOLS IN TREATING OVARIAN CANCER. THEIR OPTIMAL SEQUENCE IS DETERMINED BY DISEASE STAGE AND RESECTABILITY: IF SURGERY CANNOT BE PERFORMED FIRST DUE TO EXTENSIVE SPREAD, CHEMOTHERAPY IS ADMINISTERED BEFORE SURGERY; OTHERWISE, SURGERY IS PERFORMED FIRST AND FOLLOWED BY CHEMOTHERAPY. THE COMBINED EXPERTISE OF A GYNECOLOGIC ONCOLOGIST COVERS BOTH MODALITIES, ENSURING THAT PATIENTS RECEIVE THE MOST APPROPRIATE AND INDIVIDUALIZED TREATMENT PLAN.">
      <data key="d0">IN SUMMARY, SURGERY AND CHEMOTHERAPY ARE BOTH ESSENTIAL AND COMPLEMENTARY TOOLS IN TREATING OVARIAN CANCER. THEIR OPTIMAL SEQUENCE IS DETERMINED BY DISEASE STAGE AND RESECTABILITY: IF SURGERY CANNOT BE PERFORMED FIRST DUE TO EXTENSIVE SPREAD, CHEMOTHERAPY IS ADMINISTERED BEFORE SURGERY; OTHERWISE, SURGERY IS PERFORMED FIRST AND FOLLOWED BY CHEMOTHERAPY. THE COMBINED EXPERTISE OF A GYNECOLOGIC ONCOLOGIST COVERS BOTH MODALITIES, ENSURING THAT PATIENTS RECEIVE THE MOST APPROPRIATE AND INDIVIDUALIZED TREATMENT PLAN.</data>
      <data key="d1">IN SUMMARY, SURGERY AND CHEMOTHERAPY ARE BOTH ESSENTIAL AND COMPLEMENTARY TOOLS IN TREATING OVARIAN CANCER. THEIR OPTIMAL SEQUENCE IS DETERMINED BY DISEASE STAGE AND RESECTABILITY: IF SURGERY CANNOT BE PERFORMED FIRST DUE TO EXTENSIVE SPREAD, CHEMOTHERAPY IS ADMINISTERED BEFORE SURGERY; OTHERWISE, SURGERY IS PERFORMED FIRST AND FOLLOWED BY CHEMOTHERAPY. THE COMBINED EXPERTISE OF A GYNECOLOGIC ONCOLOGIST COVERS BOTH MODALITIES, ENSURING THAT PATIENTS RECEIVE THE MOST APPROPRIATE AND INDIVIDUALIZED TREATMENT PLAN.</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Surgery is a type of treatment, specifically a form of cancer treatment. It is one of the main treatment options available for cancer, including cervical cancer, where it is recognized as a primary treatment approach. Surgery can also be combined with other therapies; when drug therapy is administered before surgery, the pathologic stage of the disease may include a "y" prefix to indicate this sequence. As a treatment modality, surgery plays a key role in the management of cancer and is included among the main types of treatment for cervical cancer.
  - Source: TREATMENT
  - Target: SURGERY
  - Description: Surgery is a type of cancer treatment
  - Source: SURGERY
  - Target: RADIATION THERAPY
  - Description: Surgery and radiation therapy are two established cancer treatment modalities that can be used alone, together, or in sequence. Both are recognized treatment options for cancers such as thyroid cancer and squamous cell skin cancer. Their specific relationship depends on the clinical situation.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">  - Source: SURGERY
  - Target: TREATMENT
  - Description: Surgery is a type of treatment, specifically a form of cancer treatment. It is one of the main treatment options available for cancer, including cervical cancer, where it is recognized as a primary treatment approach. Surgery can also be combined with other therapies; when drug therapy is administered before surgery, the pathologic stage of the disease may include a "y" prefix to indicate this sequence. As a treatment modality, surgery plays a key role in the management of cancer and is included among the main types of treatment for cervical cancer.
  - Source: TREATMENT
  - Target: SURGERY
  - Description: Surgery is a type of cancer treatment
  - Source: SURGERY
  - Target: RADIATION THERAPY
  - Description: Surgery and radiation therapy are two established cancer treatment modalities that can be used alone, together, or in sequence. Both are recognized treatment options for cancers such as thyroid cancer and squamous cell skin cancer. Their specific relationship depends on the clinical situation.

Radiation therapy can be given before or after surgery. When given before surgery, it may help shrink a tumor before surgical removal. When given after surgery, it is often used to eliminate remaining cancer cells. In particular, radiation therapy is an option if a concerning amount of cancer remains after surgery. This makes radiation therapy an important complement to surgery, especially when surgery alone may not be sufficient.

Radiation therapy is also used as an alternative to surgery. For patients who cannot undergo surgery, or who choose not to have surgery, radiation therapy can be used instead. Therefore, while surgery and radiation therapy are sometimes combined, they can also be competing options. The descriptions are consistent: radiation therapy may be used before or after surgery; it may be added after surgery if cancer remains; it is listed as another treatment option for thyroid cancer alongside surgery; and it is a treatment option for squamous cell skin cancer along with surgery.

Ultimately, the choice between surgery and radiation therapy—or a decision to use both—depends on the type and stage of cancer, the patient's overall health, whether surgery is feasible, and patient preference. In some cases, surgery is the primary treatment, with radiation therapy used as a follow-up or backup. In other cases, radiation therapy is the primary treatment because surgery cannot be performed or is declined. Together, surgery and radiation therapy provide flexible, potentially curative, or disease-controlling options across multiple cancer types.
  - Source: RADIATION THERAPY
  - Target: SURGERY
  - Description: Radiation therapy and surgery are two distinct treatment modalities that are often used in combination for cancer care. Surgery is used to physically remove tumors, while radiation therapy uses high-energy radiation to destroy cancer cells. In many cases, treatment plans integrate both approaches: after surgery, radiation therapy is commonly administered to decrease the chance of the tumor returning and to kill any remaining cancer cells that were not removed during the operation. Additionally, radiation therapy serves as an alternative treatment option for patients who cannot undergo surgery or who choose not to have it. Thus, radiation therapy can be employed either as a standalone treatment for those ineligible for or declining surgery, or as an adjuvant therapy following surgery to improve local control and reduce the risk of recurrence. Together, surgery and radiation therapy form a complementary strategy in oncology, with the specific sequence and combination tailored to each patient's situation.
  - Source: IMAGING TESTS
  - Target: SURGERY
  - Description: Imaging tests and other testing help determine whether surgery first is possible for ovarian cancer.

ENTITY: MASTECTOMY
Description: MASTECTOMY is a surgical procedure that involves the removal of all or part of the breast. It is primarily performed as an operation to treat cancer, though it may also be used in preventive or other medical contexts. The procedure encompasses both total removal of the breast tissue and partial removal, depending on the extent of the disease and the treatment plan.
Relationships: None in the provided relationship data.

