      <data key="d6">ENTITY: SCREENING MAMMOGRAM
Description: A screening mammogram is a mammogram performed on a regular basis for routine screening purposes, specifically when there are no signs or symptoms of breast cancer. It is referenced as distinct from a diagnostic mammogram, which is typically used to evaluate abnormalities or symptoms. The screening mammogram is designed to detect breast cancer early in asymptomatic individuals, and it is a routine preventive measure.
Relationships:
  - Source: MAMMOGRAM
    Target: SCREENING MAMMOGRAM
    Description: A mammogram is a medical imaging procedure used to examine breast tissue. A screening mammogram is a specific type of mammogram that is performed regularly as a routine preventive measure, even when the individual has no symptoms or signs of breast disease. Screening mammograms are conducted on a scheduled basis, typically for early detection of breast abnormalities, including cancer, in asymptomatic individuals.
  - Source: SCREENING MAMMOGRAM
    Target: DIAGNOSTIC MAMMOGRAM
    Description: A screening mammogram is an initial imaging exam used to detect potential breast abnormalities in individuals without symptoms. When a screening mammogram reveals an abnormal area, a diagnostic mammogram is performed as a follow-up. A diagnostic mammogram is used to take a closer look at the abnormal area found on the screening mammogram, providing more detailed images to help determine whether the finding is benign or requires further investigation. Together, the screening mammogram serves as the first-line detection tool, while the diagnostic mammogram provides focused evaluation of any suspicious findings identified during screening.
  - Source: DIAGNOSTIC MAMMOGRAM
    Target: SCREENING MAMMOGRAM
    Description: A diagnostic mammogram is different from a screening mammogram
  - Source: BREAST CANCER
    Target: SCREENING MAMMOGRAM
    Description: Screening mammograms are performed routinely to look for breast cancer when there are no signs or symptoms.</data>
