      <data key="d3">Clinical staging is the rating of cancer assigned before any treatment is initiated. It is determined based on a physical exam, biopsy, and imaging tests, as well as other diagnostic evaluations. This staging system provides a baseline assessment of the extent of the disease prior to therapy. Examples of clinical staging designations include cN1, cN2, and cM0, where the prefix "c" indicates that the staging is clinical, based on initial examination and tests rather than surgical findings. The clinical stage helps guide treatment decisions and serves as a reference point for evaluating the effectiveness of subsequent interventions.
Relationships: None provided in the data tables.</data>
