    <node id="CANCER CENTER (ID: 477)">
      <data key="d0">CANCER CENTER (ID: 477)</data>
      <data key="d1">CANCER CENTER (ID: 477)</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">"A cancer center is a specialized medical organization that provides comprehensive care for patients with various types of cancer, including acute myeloid leukemia (AML) and cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC). It may offer sub-specialized expertise tailored to a patient's specific subtype of AML, ensuring that treatment plans are aligned with the individual characteristics of the disease. In addition to clinical care, cancer centers are equipped to perform advanced diagnostic imaging, such as PET-CT scans, which can be conducted using either one integrated machine or two separate devices depending on the center’s technology and workflow. Furthermore, cancer centers are recognized for their expertise in the prevention and treatment of CSCC. The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) specifically recommends that patients with certain genetic syndromes be referred to a cancer center, as these centers are well-suited to manage the complex and elevated risks associated with such conditions. Thus, a cancer center serves as a multidisciplinary facility that combines specialized disease management, advanced diagnostic capabilities, and expert guidance for high-risk patient populations."
**Relationships:** (None listed in the provided Relationships table.)

---</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: CANCER CENTER (id: 477)
Description: "A cancer center is a specialized medical organization that provides comprehensive care for patients with various types of cancer, including acute myeloid leukemia (AML) and cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC). It may offer sub-specialized expertise tailored to a patient's specific subtype of AML, ensuring that treatment plans are aligned with the individual characteristics of the disease. In addition to clinical care, cancer centers are equipped to perform advanced diagnostic imaging, such as PET-CT scans, which can be conducted using either one integrated machine or two separate devices depending on the center’s technology and workflow. Furthermore, cancer centers are recognized for their expertise in the prevention and treatment of CSCC. The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) specifically recommends that patients with certain genetic syndromes be referred to a cancer center, as these centers are well-suited to manage the complex and elevated risks associated with such conditions. Thus, a cancer center serves as a multidisciplinary facility that combines specialized disease management, advanced diagnostic capabilities, and expert guidance for high-risk patient populations."
**Relationships:** (None listed in the provided Relationships table.)

---</data>
