    <node id="SYSTEMIC THERAPY">
      <data key="d0">SYSTEMIC THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d1">SYSTEMIC THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Systemic therapy is a cancer treatment modality that works throughout the entire body rather than targeting a single area. It involves drug-based treatments—most commonly chemotherapy, targeted therapy, and immunotherapy—that travel through the bloodstream to reach cancer cells anywhere in the body. Systemic therapy is typically given intravenously, often requiring a catheter or port for delivery of medications and fluids, and oncology nurses provide hands-on administration and management of this treatment.

Systemic therapy may be used alone, in combination with radiation, or after surgery. It can be given before surgery if surgery is not possible or for advanced cancers, and it is also a treatment option if a concerning amount of cancer remains after surgery. It is a key treatment approach for metastatic cancer, including metastatic cervical cancer, where it is usually given as platinum-based chemotherapy intended to keep the cancer under control and prevent further spread. It is also used after pelvic recurrence surgery for cervical cancer.

The therapy is listed as a treatment option for several cancer types, including acute myeloid leukemia (AML), cervical cancer, squamous cell skin cancer (CSCC), thyroid cancer, and bladder cancer. For squamous cell skin cancer, systemic therapy is used when the cancer has spread or when surgery and radiation are not possible or have not been successful. For bladder cancer, it is whole-body treatment usually given intravenously, with the most common types being chemotherapy and immunotherapy.

Systemic therapy is an important option for many patients, but it can affect fertility, so this potential side effect should be considered during treatment planning. Overall, systemic therapy is a broad, body-wide cancer treatment approach that uses circulating medications to manage or control cancer in many different settings and cancer types.
Relationships: None listed</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">10. PATIENT</data>
    </node>
