    <node id="BLOOD TESTS">
      <data key="d0">BLOOD TESTS</data>
      <data key="d1">BLOOD TESTS</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">BLOOD TESTS are diagnostic and laboratory procedures performed on blood samples, widely used in cancer care and general health assessment. They provide critical information about a patient’s overall health, organ function—particularly the liver and kidneys—and the presence or extent of disease. As part of cancer evaluation, blood tests aid in diagnosis, treatment planning, and management across multiple cancer types, including ovarian cancer, liver cancer, esophageal cancer, colon cancer, and primary CNS lymphoma.

In ovarian cancer evaluation, blood tests are included among the initial laboratory studies. For liver cancer, the alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) test is a key blood test used in diagnosis and treatment planning. For colon cancer, initial testing often includes a complete blood count, chemistry profile, and carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA) test. In esophageal cancer, blood tests provide important laboratory results used in diagnosis and management. Blood tests are also part of the diagnostic workup for primary CNS lymphoma.

Blood tests are used to check for signs of disease, look for hormone secretion, and assess how well a patient is responding to cancer treatments. In thyroid cancer care, blood tests measure TSH, thyroglobulin (Tg), and anti-thyroglobulin antibodies (Tg ab) to monitor thyroid status and detect cancer recurrence. Regular TSH testing is required during thyroid replacement therapy to determine the appropriate dose of thyroid hormone. After stem cell rescue, frequent blood tests are necessary to monitor blood cell counts until the bone marrow recovers.

Blood tests are also ordered before cancer treatment to provide baseline information about general health and the function of organs such as the liver and kidneys. The results help determine the patient’s individualized treatment plan. During cancer care, patients are advised to keep copies of their blood test results and review them through the patient portal for better understanding and continuity of care.

Overall, blood tests are essential in cancer care for screening, diagnosis, treatment selection, monitoring therapeutic response, and post-treatment surveillance. They serve as a minimally invasive window into the body’s health and disease status.
Relationships:
  - Source: BLOOD TESTS
    Target: COMPLETE BLOOD COUNT AND DIFFERENTIAL
    Description: CBC and differential are blood tests
  - Source: BLOOD TESTS
    Target: COMPLETE BLOOD COUNT
    Description: Complete blood count is a common blood test ordered as part of initial testing.
  - Source: COMPLETE BLOOD COUNT
    Target: BLOOD TESTS
    Description: A complete blood count is a type of blood test described in the text.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
