In ovarian cancer evaluation, blood tests are included among the initial laboratory studies. For liver cancer, the alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) test is a key blood test used in diagnosis and treatment planning. For colon cancer, initial testing often includes a complete blood count, chemistry profile, and carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA) test. In esophageal cancer, blood tests provide important laboratory results used in diagnosis and management. Blood tests are also part of the diagnostic workup for primary CNS lymphoma.

Blood tests are used to check for signs of disease, look for hormone secretion, and assess how well a patient is responding to cancer treatments. In thyroid cancer care, blood tests measure TSH, thyroglobulin (Tg), and anti-thyroglobulin antibodies (Tg ab) to monitor thyroid status and detect cancer recurrence. Regular TSH testing is required during thyroid replacement therapy to determine the appropriate dose of thyroid hormone. After stem cell rescue, frequent blood tests are necessary to monitor blood cell counts until the bone marrow recovers.

