      <data key="d1">ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is a fast-growing blood cancer that originates in lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell. In this disease, the bone marrow produces an excessive number of immature lymphocytes called lymphoblasts, which crowd out healthy cells and make it difficult for the bone marrow and blood to perform their normal functions. A diagnosis of ALL requires the presence of 20% or more lymphoblasts in the bone marrow. ALL has multiple subtypes, including B-cell and T-cell ALL, which are classified based on the specific type of lymphocyte affected. It is the most common cancer diagnosed in children, though it can also affect adults. Beyond the bone marrow and blood, ALL can spread to other organs, including the testicles and the central nervous system.
Relationships: No relationships listed in the provided data.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA
Description: Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is a fast-growing blood cancer that originates in lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell. In this disease, the bone marrow produces an excessive number of immature lymphocytes called lymphoblasts, which crowd out healthy cells and make it difficult for the bone marrow and blood to perform their normal functions. A diagnosis of ALL requires the presence of 20% or more lymphoblasts in the bone marrow. ALL has multiple subtypes, including B-cell and T-cell ALL, which are classified based on the specific type of lymphocyte affected. It is the most common cancer diagnosed in children, though it can also affect adults. Beyond the bone marrow and blood, ALL can spread to other organs, including the testicles and the central nervous system.
Relationships: No relationships listed in the provided data.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GENERAL HEALTH TESTS">
      <data key="d0">GENERAL HEALTH TESTS</data>
      <data key="d1">GENERAL HEALTH TESTS</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">General health tests are a category of medical tests and examinations used to assess overall health. They are standard health assessments that typically include a medical history, family history, tobacco and alcohol use history, and a physical exam. These tests are often described as routine tests and are used as a baseline to evaluate a patient’s general condition.

General health tests play an important role in cancer care. They are performed during the diagnostic workup for nasopharyngeal carcinoma (NPC) to assess overall health. They are also used as baseline tests during liver cancer diagnosis. Similarly, they are part of the diagnostic workup for esophageal cancer, helping to evaluate the patient’s overall health, and they are included in the testing process for patients with basal cell skin cancer. In addition, these tests are commonly used to evaluate a patient’s overall health before or during cancer treatment more broadly.

The descriptions also indicate that general health tests are mentioned in a patient guide, where the text directs readers to see the "Guide General health tests" for possible tests. This suggests they are considered a reference category within medical guidance materials.

Overall, general health tests serve as a foundational assessment tool in both general medical practice and oncology, helping clinicians determine a patient’s baseline health status, guide diagnostic and treatment decisions, and monitor well-being throughout the cancer journey.
Relationships: No relationships listed in the provided data.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
