    <node id="IMMUNOSUPPRESSANTS** — THESE INCLUDE CORTICOSTEROIDS (E.G., PREDNISONE), CALCINEURIN INHIBITORS (E.G., CYCLOSPORINE, TACROLIMUS), AND ANTIMETABOLITES (E.G., AZATHIOPRINE, MYCOPHENOLATE). THEY WORK BY SUPPRESSING THE OVERACTIVE IMMUNE RESPONSE: CORTICOSTEROIDS BIND TO GLUCOCORTICOID RECEPTORS TO INHIBIT PRO-INFLAMMATORY GENE TRANSCRIPTION; CALCINEURIN INHIBITORS BLOCK T-CELL ACTIVATION BY PREVENTING NFAT (NUCLEAR FACTOR OF ACTIVATED T-CELLS) FROM ENTERING THE NUCLEUS.">
      <data key="d0">IMMUNOSUPPRESSANTS** — THESE INCLUDE CORTICOSTEROIDS (E.G., PREDNISONE), CALCINEURIN INHIBITORS (E.G., CYCLOSPORINE, TACROLIMUS), AND ANTIMETABOLITES (E.G., AZATHIOPRINE, MYCOPHENOLATE). THEY WORK BY SUPPRESSING THE OVERACTIVE IMMUNE RESPONSE: CORTICOSTEROIDS BIND TO GLUCOCORTICOID RECEPTORS TO INHIBIT PRO-INFLAMMATORY GENE TRANSCRIPTION; CALCINEURIN INHIBITORS BLOCK T-CELL ACTIVATION BY PREVENTING NFAT (NUCLEAR FACTOR OF ACTIVATED T-CELLS) FROM ENTERING THE NUCLEUS.</data>
      <data key="d1">IMMUNOSUPPRESSANTS** — THESE INCLUDE CORTICOSTEROIDS (E.G., PREDNISONE), CALCINEURIN INHIBITORS (E.G., CYCLOSPORINE, TACROLIMUS), AND ANTIMETABOLITES (E.G., AZATHIOPRINE, MYCOPHENOLATE). THEY WORK BY SUPPRESSING THE OVERACTIVE IMMUNE RESPONSE: CORTICOSTEROIDS BIND TO GLUCOCORTICOID RECEPTORS TO INHIBIT PRO-INFLAMMATORY GENE TRANSCRIPTION; CALCINEURIN INHIBITORS BLOCK T-CELL ACTIVATION BY PREVENTING NFAT (NUCLEAR FACTOR OF ACTIVATED T-CELLS) FROM ENTERING THE NUCLEUS.</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3" />
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">
**2. Disease-Modifying Antirheumatic Drugs (DMARDs)** — Conventional DMARDs such as methotrexate, hydroxychloroquine, and sulfasalazine reduce inflammation and immune cell proliferation by interfering with folate metabolism, purine synthesis, and antigen presentation.

**3. Biologic Agents** — These include TNF inhibitors (e.g., infliximab, etanercept), IL-6 inhibitors (e.g., tocilizumab), and B-cell–depleting agents such as **rituximab**. Notably, the data describes rituximab as a monoclonal antibody that attaches to CD20 proteins on cells, enabling the immune system to destroy them [Data: Entities (175, 1104, 1105)]. In autoimmune diseases such as rheumatoid arthritis and lupus, rituximab depletes pathogenic B cells through this same CD20-targeted mechanism. TNF inhibitors neutralize tumor necrosis factor-alpha, a key pro-inflammatory cytokine driving autoimmune inflammation.

**4. Targeted Synthetic DMARDs (JAK Inhibitors)** — Drugs such as tofacitinib and baricitinib block Janus kinase (JAK)–STAT signaling pathways, which are used by many cytokines to propagate inflammation in autoimmune diseases.

**5. Intravenous Immunoglobulin (IVIG) and Plasmapheresis** — IVIG provides neutralizing antibodies and modulates Fc receptors to dampen autoantibody activity; plasmapheresis physically removes pathogenic autoantibodies and inflammatory proteins from circulation.

**6. Anti-Inflammatory Agents (NSAIDs)** — Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs reduce pain and inflammation by inhibiting cyclooxygenase (COX-1/COX-2) enzymes, thereby decreasing prostaglandin production.
</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RELATIONSHIPS: NONE LISTED">
