      <data key="d1">RELATIONSHIPS: NONE LISTED</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Radiation therapy is a cancer treatment that uses high-energy waves, such as X-rays, photons, protons, and other sources of radiation, to kill cancer cells and shrink tumors. It is administered by radiation oncologists, who are experts in this treatment, and includes two main types: external beam radiation therapy and internal radiation therapy (brachytherapy). As a local treatment, it affects a specific area of the body and may be combined with chemotherapy or other systemic therapies to enhance its effect. It can be given alone, before or after surgery, as adjuvant therapy to kill remaining cancer cells, or as primary treatment when surgery is not possible or is declined. It is also used when a concerning amount of cancer remains after surgery, and for unresectable cancers, sometimes with chemotherapy and systemic therapy.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">
ENTITY: RADIATION THERAPY
Description: Radiation therapy is a cancer treatment that uses high-energy waves, such as X-rays, photons, protons, and other sources of radiation, to kill cancer cells and shrink tumors. It is administered by radiation oncologists, who are experts in this treatment, and includes two main types: external beam radiation therapy and internal radiation therapy (brachytherapy). As a local treatment, it affects a specific area of the body and may be combined with chemotherapy or other systemic therapies to enhance its effect. It can be given alone, before or after surgery, as adjuvant therapy to kill remaining cancer cells, or as primary treatment when surgery is not possible or is declined. It is also used when a concerning amount of cancer remains after surgery, and for unresectable cancers, sometimes with chemotherapy and systemic therapy.

Radiation therapy is used to treat many cancer types. It is a treatment option for prostate cancer, and may be avoided or delayed if molecular biomarker testing indicates lower-risk disease. For rectal cancer, it is often used and can damage the hormone-making function of the ovaries and testicles, potentially causing infertility; fertility preservation should be considered beforehand. For anal cancer, radiation therapy is almost always part of initial treatment, often combined with chemotherapy, and is given in daily fractions over 5 to 6 weeks, beginning with the first 5-FU infusion. It can also be used for recurrent inguinal lymph nodes if not previously irradiated. In anal cancer treatment, it can cause tissue damage and bowel, bladder, and sexual side effects, and may weaken or damage the perineum, affecting healing after abdominoperineal resection (APR). For cervical cancer, radiation therapy is a treatment category that includes external beam radiation therapy and brachytherapy. It is a treatment option for squamous cell skin cancer when surgery is not an option, after surgery, or with systemic therapy. It is also listed for thyroid cancer. For primary CNS lymphoma, radiation therapy is a main treatment that can slow tumor growth and reduce symptoms; whole-brain radiation therapy is the most commonly used technique. It can be delivered to the brain and sometimes expanded to include the eyes, with side effects including headaches, nausea, fatigue, and possible long-term nervous system damage. For head and neck cancers, radiation can affect endocrine glands such as the thyroid and pituitary, as well as hearing, and may require related assessments before or after treatment.

Because radiation therapy affects tissues, the same area generally cannot be retreated after an initial course of radiation.
Relationships: None listed

ENTITY: DISEASE
Description: Disease is a condition that lymph nodes and immune cells help the body fight
Relationships: None listed

ENTITY: MONOCLONAL ANTIBODY THERAPY
Description: Monoclonal antibody therapy is a type of immunotherapy that hunts for specific proteins on cancer cells. Rituximab is a monoclonal antibody used in this treatment.
Relationships: None listed

ENTITY: U.S. FDA
Description: The U.S. FDA, also known as the U.S. Food and Drug Administration, is a federal agency of the United States government. As a government organization, it is responsible for regulating and approving medical treatments, including investigational treatments that arise from clinical trials. The agency evaluates drugs, devices, and treatment approaches and grants approval once they are found to be both safe and effective in clinical trials. In addition to standard approvals, the U.S. FDA may also approve investigational treatments derived from clinical trials, ensuring that new medical interventions meet rigorous standards before reaching patients.
Relationships: None listed

ENTITY: INFECTIONS
Description: Infections are illnesses caused by pathogens, and they are commonly included in family history questions as part of medical assessments. In the context of cancer treatment, infections are also a frequent side effect of targeted therapy, and the body becomes particularly vulnerable to infections during high-dose chemotherapy. Thus, infections represent both a general category of disease and a specific clinical concern for patients undergoing certain cancer treatments.
Relationships: None listed

ENTITY: TARGETED THERAPY
Description: Targeted therapy is a type of systemic cancer treatment that uses drugs to attack specific parts of cancer cells, such as genetic mutations, biomarkers, receptors, or proteins, in order to kill the cells or slow their growth and spread. As a systemic therapy, it travels throughout the body, and it is designed to target cancer cells specifically, often sparing normal cells more than traditional chemotherapy. It is frequently used for people whose cancers harbor particular gene mutations or express specific molecular markers.

Targeted therapy is employed across a range of cancers. In cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC), it may be used when the disease has spread, especially if surgery and radiation are not possible or unsuccessful; this includes EGFR inhibitors that target receptors on cancer cells. In acute myeloid leukemia (AML), targeted therapy is a treatment option that targets specific cancer features, and the care team may discuss it as one possible approach. For primary CNS lymphoma, targeted therapy attacks specific parts of cancer cells to kill them or slow their growth, can be combined with chemotherapy, and may have fewer side effects than chemotherapy alone. In prostate cancer, targeted therapy can target cancer-related proteins such as PSMA, and PSMA-PET imaging may help determine whether targeted therapy could be effective for certain metastatic cases.

Targeted therapy is also mentioned as a possible treatment option in clinical trials, reflecting its evolving role in oncology. Because it is a systemic treatment, it can affect fertility or the ability to have children, a consideration that patients and care teams must take into account. Overall, targeted therapy represents a precision medicine approach, offering a treatment strategy tailored to the specific molecular characteristics of an individual’s cancer. It can be used alone or in combination with other treatments such as chemotherapy, and it may offer a more focused alternative when conventional approaches like surgery and radiation are not viable. The descriptions consistently characterize targeted therapy as a systemic, cell-specific treatment approach, with no contradictions among the provided details. Its precise mechanism and applicability vary by cancer type, but the unifying principle is the use of drugs to intervene in specific biological pathways that drive cancer growth, thereby providing a more personalized and often better-tolerated treatment option.
Relationships: None listed

ENTITY: SYSTEMIC THERAPY
Description: Systemic therapy is a cancer treatment modality that works throughout the entire body rather than targeting a single area. It involves drug-based treatments—most commonly chemotherapy, targeted therapy, and immunotherapy—that travel through the bloodstream to reach cancer cells anywhere in the body. Systemic therapy is typically given intravenously, often requiring a catheter or port for delivery of medications and fluids, and oncology nurses provide hands-on administration and management of this treatment.

Systemic therapy may be used alone, in combination with radiation, or after surgery. It can be given before surgery if surgery is not possible or for advanced cancers, and it is also a treatment option if a concerning amount of cancer remains after surgery. It is a key treatment approach for metastatic cancer, including metastatic cervical cancer, where it is usually given as platinum-based chemotherapy intended to keep the cancer under control and prevent further spread. It is also used after pelvic recurrence surgery for cervical cancer.

The therapy is listed as a treatment option for several cancer types, including acute myeloid leukemia (AML), cervical cancer, squamous cell skin cancer (CSCC), thyroid cancer, and bladder cancer. For squamous cell skin cancer, systemic therapy is used when the cancer has spread or when surgery and radiation are not possible or have not been successful. For bladder cancer, it is whole-body treatment usually given intravenously, with the most common types being chemotherapy and immunotherapy.

Systemic therapy is an important option for many patients, but it can affect fertility, so this potential side effect should be considered during treatment planning. Overall, systemic therapy is a broad, body-wide cancer treatment approach that uses circulating medications to manage or control cancer in many different settings and cancer types.
Relationships: None listed

ENTITY: CHEMOTHERAPY
Description: Chemotherapy is a cancer treatment that uses medicines or drugs to kill cancer cells, primarily by damaging and destroying rapidly dividing cells throughout the body. It is most often a systemic therapy, meaning the drugs travel through the bloodstream to reach cells everywhere in the body, but it can also be delivered locally—for example, applied directly to the skin as a topical cream (such as 5-fluorouracil), delivered into the bladder (intravesically), or given by mouth, injection, or intravenous infusion. Chemotherapy is typically given in cycles of treatment days followed by rest days, and it may be used as a single agent or as a multi-agent regimen. It can be administered before surgery (neoadjuvant therapy) to shrink tumors, after surgery (adjuvant therapy) to eliminate remaining cancer cells, or as the primary treatment. When combined with radiation, it is called chemoradiation; it may also be given after chemoradiation to kill lingering cancer cells.

Chemotherapy is the standard-of-care systemic treatment for acute myeloid leukemia (AML), where it kills fast-dividing cells and is often multi-agent and given in cycles. It is also a main treatment for primary CNS lymphoma, with methotrexate being the principal drug; it can be combined with targeted therapy, and high-dose chemotherapy may be part of a stem cell rescue. For cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC) that has spread, chemotherapy may be used when surgery and radiation are not possible or unsuccessful. In colorectal cancer, chemotherapy is recommended after colectomy for all stage 3 and some stage 2 colon cancers, and it is useful for pMMR/MSS cancers when given first; rectal cancer regimens may include oxaliplatin or irinotecan. For ovarian cancer, chemotherapy may be given first when surgery is not initially possible (with preferred regimens including paclitaxel and carboplatin), or after surgery to address residual disease. For cervical cancer, chemotherapy is combined with radiation as chemoradiation, often given once weekly. For anal cancer, chemotherapy is used together with radiation, and also for cancer that returns to lymph nodes or becomes metastatic. For bladder cancer, chemotherapy is one of the main intravesical therapies (delivered directly into the bladder), and may also be given systemically before or after surgery.

Side effects of chemotherapy can include nausea, vomiting, fatigue, fever, infection, tingling or numbness, rash, and diarrhea. It can also temporarily or permanently affect fertility, depending on the patient's age, treatment type, dose, and duration; therefore, fertility preservation may need to be considered before starting treatment. A medical oncologist is the specialist who treats cancer with chemotherapy. If chemotherapy is planned as the first treatment, a biopsy is typically performed before therapy begins. Common chemotherapy drugs include platinum-based agents such as cisplatin and carboplatin, as well as 5-FU, mitomycin, capecitabine, oxaliplatin, irinotecan, and methotrexate.
Relationships: None listed

---

