      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6"> The screening relationship to CANCER TREATMENT states: "Screening is needed because some cancer treatments can reactivate hepatitis B or hepatitis C and cause liver damage" [Data: Relationships (1062); Sources (84)].</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PATIENTS WITH CHRONIC LYMPHOCYTIC LEUKEMIA (CLL).">
      <data key="d0">PATIENTS WITH CHRONIC LYMPHOCYTIC LEUKEMIA (CLL).</data>
      <data key="d1">PATIENTS WITH CHRONIC LYMPHOCYTIC LEUKEMIA (CLL).</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3" />
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6"> Hepatitis B and C testing is explicitly performed before CLL treatment, and hepatitis B is described as a *required* screening test before CLL treatment due to the risk of viral reactivation during immunosuppressive therapy; hepatitis C is likewise specifically tested for before CLL treatment begins [Data: Entities (245, 246); Relationships (467); Sources (30)].</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PATIENTS UNDERGOING TREATMENT PLANNING GENERALLY.">
      <data key="d0">PATIENTS UNDERGOING TREATMENT PLANNING GENERALLY.</data>
      <data key="d1">PATIENTS UNDERGOING TREATMENT PLANNING GENERALLY.</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3" />
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6"> Screening for hepatitis B is listed as an as-needed part of treatment planning, and the resulting hepatitis blood test informs the **treatment team** whether the patient has or had hepatitis B or C [Data: Relationships (1061, 2156); Sources (159, 84)].

More broadly, hepatitis B and hepatitis C are themselves significant because they can cause chronic hepatitis, cirrhosis, liver failure, and liver cancer — the underlying rationale for screening before immunosuppressive or hepatotoxic cancer therapy [Data: Entities (245, 246); Relationships (735, 738, 736, 739, 737, 740); Sources (51, 84)].

---
</data>
    </node>
