      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Pelvic magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is an imaging test recommended for sessile polyps and for staging rectal cancer; it shows the extent of cancer in the rectum and nearby lymph nodes.
Relationships:
  - Source: POLYPECTOMY
    Target: PELVIC MAGNETIC RESONANCE IMAGING
    Description: Pelvic MRI is recommended for some polyps, especially sessile polyps, to help guide decisions about surgery after polypectomy.
  - Source: ENDOSCOPIC ULTRASOUND
    Target: PELVIC MAGNETIC RESONANCE IMAGING
    Description: Both are imaging options recommended for sessile polyps to help guide decisions about surgery.
  - Source: PELVIC MAGNETIC RESONANCE IMAGING
    Target: CLINICAL PRE-SURGERY STAGE
    Description: Pelvic MRI results help determine the clinical/pre-surgery stage of rectal cancer.
  - Source: PELVIC MAGNETIC RESONANCE IMAGING
    Target: CT OF THE CHEST AND ABDOMEN
    Description: Both are imaging tests used in rectal cancer staging: pelvic MRI shows extent in the rectum and nearby lymph nodes, and chest/abdomen CT checks for spread to the liver, lungs, or other organs.

