      <data key="d3">MSI-H/dMMR mutation is a tumor characteristic defined by the presence of high microsatellite instability (MSI-H) or deficient mismatch repair (dMMR). This mutation status occurs when errors or defects in the DNA mismatch repair system are present and are not corrected, leading to the accumulation of mutations, particularly in microsatellite regions of the genome. As a result, tumors with MSI-H/dMMR exhibit a distinct genetic profile that is clinically significant. This mutation status is relevant to biomarker testing, as it helps identify patients who may respond to certain treatments. Specifically, pembrolizumab, an immune checkpoint inhibitor, is often used to treat tumors that are MSI-H or dMMR, regardless of their location in the body. In summary, MSI-H/dMMR mutation represents a key predictive biomarker in oncology, guiding treatment decisions and enabling targeted immunotherapy for affected patients.
Relationships:
  - Source: MICROSATELLITE INSTABILITY (MSI)
    Target: MSI-H/DMMR MUTATION
    Description: MSI-H is high microsatellite instability, often due to dMMR genes, and is part of the MSI-H/dMMR tumor mutation status.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
