    Description: If APR is planned, cancerous lymph nodes are removed during APR; otherwise a standalone lymph node dissection may be done

ENTITY: GENETIC TESTING
Description: Genetic testing is a medical procedure that analyzes genes or DNA to identify mutations, particularly those associated with cancer risk. It is closely related to biomarker testing and can be performed on blood, urine, saliva, or tumor samples to look for both inherited (germline) and tumor-related (somatic) mutations. When testing for inherited risk, it examines normal cells throughout the body to detect germline or inherited cancer-risk mutations, including in genes such as BRCA and other hereditary cancer genes. This type of testing is often recommended for individuals with a family history of cancer, and it is universally recommended for everyone diagnosed with ovarian cancer.

In clinical practice, genetic testing for inherited mutations is an important part of evaluating suspected ovarian cancer, often listed alongside family history. Patients are advised to know whether family members have undergone genetic testing, as well as the exact types and locations of cancers in their family, since this information can guide testing decisions. The care team typically asks whether family members have had genetic testing to better interpret results and assess hereditary risk. Testing for inherited genetic variants can be relevant when family members have had cancer, helping to identify individuals at increased risk.

In addition to inherited risk assessment, genetic testing may also be performed on a biopsy sample to help diagnose certain types of cancer, such as follicular or oncocytic thyroid cancer, when pathologic examination of removed tissue is not definitive. It is also used to test tumor tissue directly to identify mutations that may guide treatment decisions. The scope of genetic testing therefore encompasses both hereditary gene testing and tumor testing, covering germline and somatic alterations. By analyzing DNA from various sample types, genetic testing provides a comprehensive picture of cancer risk and tumor biology. It is a key tool for identifying inherited mutations that increase cancer risk and for informing personalized medical management. Overall, genetic testing serves multiple purposes: confirming hereditary cancer syndromes, guiding cancer diagnosis when standard pathology is inconclusive, and supporting treatment planning through tumor profiling. Because family history plays a crucial role, genetic testing is most effective when patients have accurate knowledge of their relatives’ cancer diagnoses and prior genetic testing results.
Relationships:
  - Source: GENETIC TESTING
    Target: GERMLINE TESTING
    Description: Genetic testing is a broad term that encompasses various methods used to analyze an individual's DNA. Germline testing is a specific type of genetic testing that focuses on identifying inherited mutations. These mutations are present from birth and can be passed down from parents to their children. By looking for these inherited changes, germline testing helps assess an individual's risk for certain genetic conditions and can inform medical decisions.
  - Source: GENETIC TESTING
    Target: LYNCH SYNDROME
    Description: Genetic testing can identify Lynch syndrome and other hereditary syndromes
  - Source: GENETIC TESTING
    Target: HEREDITARY OVARIAN CANCER
    Description: Genetic testing can identify inherited mutations that cause hereditary ovarian cancer
  - Source: GENETIC TESTING
    Target: HEREDITARY GENE TESTING
    Description: Hereditary gene testing is one of the two basic types of genetic testing for prostate cancer care

ENTITY: MSI-H/DMMR MUTATION
Description: MSI-H/dMMR mutation is a tumor characteristic defined by the presence of high microsatellite instability (MSI-H) or deficient mismatch repair (dMMR). This mutation status occurs when errors or defects in the DNA mismatch repair system are present and are not corrected, leading to the accumulation of mutations, particularly in microsatellite regions of the genome. As a result, tumors with MSI-H/dMMR exhibit a distinct genetic profile that is clinically significant. This mutation status is relevant to biomarker testing, as it helps identify patients who may respond to certain treatments. Specifically, pembrolizumab, an immune checkpoint inhibitor, is often used to treat tumors that are MSI-H or dMMR, regardless of their location in the body. In summary, MSI-H/dMMR mutation represents a key predictive biomarker in oncology, guiding treatment decisions and enabling targeted immunotherapy for affected patients.
Relationships:
  - Source: MICROSATELLITE INSTABILITY (MSI)
    Target: MSI-H/DMMR MUTATION
    Description: MSI-H is high microsatellite instability, often due to dMMR genes, and is part of the MSI-H/dMMR tumor mutation status.

ENTITY: GERMLINE MUTATION
Description: A germline mutation is a gene mutation inherited from a birth parent. It is an inherited mutation present in every cell of the body and is passed down in families, which is why it is also called a hereditary mutation. Germline mutations can increase an individual's cancer risk, can be passed on to children, and may also be present in blood relatives.
Relationships:
  - Source: GERMLINE TESTING
    Target: GERMLINE MUTATION
    Description: Germline testing looks for germline mutations in every cell.
  - Source: GERMLINE MUTATION
    Target: HEREDITARY GENE TESTING
    Description: Hereditary gene testing looks for inherited germline mutations in every cell

ENTITY: MISMATCH REPAIR TESTING
Description: Mismatch repair testing is a laboratory analysis performed on a sample of colon tumor tissue. It is used to detect the presence of mismatch repair deficiency (dMMR) or high microsatellite instability (MSI-H) biomarkers. This testing is recommended for everyone diagnosed with colon cancer, as it provides important information about the tumor's characteristics.
Relationships:
  - Source: MISMATCH REPAIR TESTING
    Target: LYNCH SYNDROME
    Description: If colon cancer is dMMR/MSI-H, mismatch repair testing leads to testing for Lynch syndrome
  - Source: MISMATCH REPAIR TESTING
    Target: MICROSATELLITE INSTABILITY TESTING
    Description: Mismatch repair testing and microsatellite instability testing are recommended methods for evaluating colon cancer biomarkers

---
