    <node id="MUSCLE FLAP RECONSTRUCTION">
      <data key="d0">MUSCLE FLAP RECONSTRUCTION</data>
      <data key="d1">MUSCLE FLAP RECONSTRUCTION</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Muscle flap reconstruction is a reconstructive surgical procedure that involves transferring muscle tissue from one part of the body, most commonly the rectus abdominis muscle, to another area to support healing, restore structure, or rebuild lost tissue. In one context, it is performed as a reconstructive surgery event specifically to improve healing of the perineum after an abdominoperineal resection (APR). In another context, the term is used as another name for vaginal reconstruction, in which muscle from another area—often the rectus abdominis—is employed to reconstruct or support the vaginal region. Both descriptions refer to the same underlying technique: using a muscle flap to aid in surgical reconstruction, with the rectus abdominis as a frequent donor site. There is no contradiction between the two descriptions; they highlight different applications of the same procedure. Thus, muscle flap reconstruction is a versatile surgical approach used for perineal healing after APR and for vaginal reconstruction, among other reconstructive needs.
**Relationships:**
  - Source: VAGINAL RECONSTRUCTION
    Target: MUSCLE FLAP RECONSTRUCTION
    Description: Muscle flap reconstruction is another term for vaginal reconstruction using a muscle from another area of the body
  - Source: ABDOMINOPERINEAL RESECTION (APR)
    Target: MUSCLE FLAP RECONSTRUCTION
    Description: Muscle flap reconstruction can improve healing of the perineum and reduce complications after APR

---</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: MUSCLE FLAP RECONSTRUCTION
Description: Muscle flap reconstruction is a reconstructive surgical procedure that involves transferring muscle tissue from one part of the body, most commonly the rectus abdominis muscle, to another area to support healing, restore structure, or rebuild lost tissue. In one context, it is performed as a reconstructive surgery event specifically to improve healing of the perineum after an abdominoperineal resection (APR). In another context, the term is used as another name for vaginal reconstruction, in which muscle from another area—often the rectus abdominis—is employed to reconstruct or support the vaginal region. Both descriptions refer to the same underlying technique: using a muscle flap to aid in surgical reconstruction, with the rectus abdominis as a frequent donor site. There is no contradiction between the two descriptions; they highlight different applications of the same procedure. Thus, muscle flap reconstruction is a versatile surgical approach used for perineal healing after APR and for vaginal reconstruction, among other reconstructive needs.
**Relationships:**
  - Source: VAGINAL RECONSTRUCTION
