    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="ULTRASOUND" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d8">ULTRASOUND</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Ultrasound plays a key role in biopsy procedures, serving both as a guidance tool and as a diagnostic aid. During a biopsy, ultrasound can be used to guide the needle to the precise location of a target tissue, improving accuracy and safety. This guidance is especially useful for lymph node biopsies, where ultrasound helps direct the procedure to the affected node. Additionally, ultrasound results help determine whether a needle biopsy is needed for a thyroid nodule, as imaging findings can indicate whether sampling is necessary. Thus, ultrasound supports biopsy decision-making and execution across different clinical contexts.</data>
      <data key="d12">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="OPEN BIOPSY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d8">OPEN BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Open biopsy is a type of biopsy, which is a medical procedure used to obtain tissue samples for diagnosis. Specifically, open biopsy is recognized as one of the standard types of biopsy for gliomas, a form of brain tumor. Thus, within the broader category of biopsy, open biopsy holds particular importance in the diagnostic evaluation of gliomas.</data>
      <data key="d12">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="CHEMOTHERAPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">If chemotherapy is planned first, a biopsy is performed to remove a sample of the tumor before treatment.</data>
      <data key="d12">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="TREATMENT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Biopsy results and biopsy findings play a central role in guiding cancer treatment decisions. After a cancer diagnosis, these results are used to help plan the appropriate treatment approach. Additionally, biopsy findings provide specific information about which treatments the cancer may respond to, enabling more targeted and effective therapy. Together, biopsy and treatment are directly connected: the biopsy supplies essential diagnostic and predictive data, while treatment planning relies on that data to determine the best course of action for each patient.</data>
      <data key="d12">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
