    <edge source="BREAST CANCER" target="DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">BREAST CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Ductal carcinoma in situ (DCIS) is a form of breast cancer, specifically classified as a noninvasive, stage 0 condition. As a breast cancer subtype, DCIS is characterized by the presence of abnormal cells that are confined within the milk ducts of the breast and have not spread into surrounding breast tissue. This noninvasive nature distinguishes it from invasive breast cancers, and it is considered the earliest stage of breast cancer development.</data>
      <data key="d12">DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BREAST CANCER" target="INVASIVE BREAST CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">BREAST CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">INVASIVE BREAST CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">BREAST CANCER encompasses various forms of malignancy originating in the breast. INVASIVE BREAST CANCER is a specific form of breast cancer. It is characterized by cancer that has spread from the ducts or lobules into the surrounding breast tissue or nearby lymph nodes. In other words, invasive breast cancer is a category of breast cancer in which malignant cells have broken out of their original sites within the milk ducts or lobules and invaded the adjacent breast tissue, with the potential to reach nearby lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d12">BREAST CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BREAST CANCER" target="NEEDLE BIOPSY" id="related_to">
