    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="CANCER STAGING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">TREATMENT</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER STAGING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Treatment decisions are guided by cancer stage, and treatment may begin after staging is initially determined.</data>
      <data key="d12">CANCER STAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">TREATMENT</data>
      <data key="d8">PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Primary CNS lymphoma can be treated to slow growth, reduce symptoms, and prolong life</data>
      <data key="d12">TREATMENT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="CARE TEAM" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">TREATMENT</data>
      <data key="d8">CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The care team is central to the entire cancer treatment process, particularly for patients with acute myeloid leukemia (AML). Their responsibilities begin with planning the next steps of care, which are determined based on imaging and test results. Using these findings, the care team identifies the appropriate course of action and outlines the treatment pathway.

The care team provides treatment options and comprehensive care to the patient. Treatment planning is a collaborative process: the patient and care team together choose a treatment plan that aligns with the patients needs and preferences. Once a plan is agreed upon, the care team coordinates care throughout the entire treatment journey, ensuring that all aspects of the plan are implemented smoothly.

In addition to planning, the care team actively prescribes and monitors treatments. They ensure that each treatment is properly initiated, and they list the start date of each treatment. This monitoring is essential for tracking the patients response and adjusting the plan as needed. Ultimately, the care team provides the planned cancer treatment itself, delivering the therapies that have been selected for the patient. Through this combination of planning, prescribing, monitoring, coordinating, and providing care, the care team manages the full continuum of AML treatment, supporting the patient from initial decision-making through ongoing therapy.</data>
      <data key="d12">CARE TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TREATMENT" target="DOCTOR" id="related_to">
