    <edge source="MRI" target="BRAIN MRI" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">BRAIN MRI</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Brain MRI is an MRI application for the brain</data>
      <data key="d12">BRAIN MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="SPINE MRI" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">SPINE MRI</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Spine MRI is an MRI application for the spine</data>
      <data key="d12">SPINE MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="BREAST MRI" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">BREAST MRI</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Breast MRI is an MRI application for the breast</data>
      <data key="d12">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">MRI is an imaging test used to diagnose primary CNS lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d12">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="BIOPSY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">MRI (magnetic resonance imaging) can play a key role in biopsy procedures. It is capable of revealing suspicious areas within the body that may require sampling by biopsy. Additionally, MRI can be used to guide a biopsy, helping to ensure that the procedure targets the identified area accurately. Thus, MRI serves both as a detection tool and as a guidance method for biopsy.</data>
      <data key="d12">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="SURGERY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">SURGERY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">If surgery is needed or being considered, MRI is ordered to evaluate the depth and spread of rectal cancer</data>
      <data key="d12">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="CARE TEAM" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The care team relies on MRI scans as a key diagnostic and monitoring tool throughout the patients journey, including during diagnosis, treatment, and follow-up care. Because the MRI procedure involves being inside an enclosed scanner, the care team should be informed if the patient feels nervous or anxious in enclosed spaces before the scan takes place. This allows the team to make appropriate accommodations and ensure the patients comfort and safety.</data>
      <data key="d12">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="CT SCAN" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">CT SCAN</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">MRI and CT scan are both medical imaging modalities used to visualize internal structures for diagnostic purposes. They are similar in that each may involve the use of a contrast agent to enhance image quality, but the contrast materials used for MRI and CT scan are different. An MRI scan, while similar to a CT scan, generally takes longer and uses a more enclosed machine, which can feel more confining to patients. CT scans are often employed as an alternative when MRI is unavailable or is not recommended for a particular patient. Conversely, MRI may be used to investigate cancer in the abdomen, especially in the liver, when a patient cannot receive the contrast agent typically used for a CT scan. In such cases, MRI offers a viable diagnostic option with a contrast agent that the patient can tolerate. Overall, the two tests complement each other: CT scans provide a quicker and more accessible imaging method, while MRI offers detailed imaging with a different contrast agent profile, making it useful when CT contrast is contraindicated or when MRI is otherwise preferable.</data>
      <data key="d12">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="PET SCAN" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">PET SCAN</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">PET scans may be combined with MRI for imaging.</data>
      <data key="d12">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="ULTRASOUND" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">ULTRASOUND</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">MRI and ultrasound are imaging tests used in breast cancer evaluation</data>
      <data key="d12">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="FERTILITY-SPARING TREATMENT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">FERTILITY-SPARING TREATMENT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">MRI of the pelvis is recommended 6 months after fertility-sparing surgery and yearly for 2 to 3 years.</data>
      <data key="d12">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="ENDORECTAL ULTRASOUND" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">ENDORECTAL ULTRASOUND</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Endorectal ultrasound may be done instead of pelvic MRI when MRI cannot be used or its images are unhelpful</data>
      <data key="d12">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="POSITRON EMISSION TOMOGRAPHY (PET)" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">POSITRON EMISSION TOMOGRAPHY (PET)</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">PET can be combined with MRI for imaging</data>
      <data key="d12">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="CLOSED MRI" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">CLOSED MRI</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Closed MRI is a type of MRI with a capsule-like design</data>
      <data key="d12">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="TRANSVAGINAL ULTRASOUND" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">TRANSVAGINAL ULTRASOUND</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Transvaginal ultrasound may be used when MRI of the pelvis cannot be done</data>
      <data key="d12">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="UROGRAM" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">UROGRAM</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A urogram can be performed using MRI to evaluate the urinary tract.</data>
      <data key="d12">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="OPEN MRI" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">OPEN MRI</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Open MRI is a type of MRI with a magnetic top and bottom</data>
      <data key="d12">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CARE TEAM" target="HEALTH CARE PROVIDER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d8">HEALTH CARE PROVIDER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The care team is a collaborative group that includes health care providers as integral members. Health care providers are part of the care team, serving as members who contribute to the team's overall function. In essence, the health care provider is a constituent of the care team, with both descriptions emphasizing that providers belong to and participate within the care team structure.</data>
      <data key="d12">HEALTH CARE PROVIDER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CARE TEAM" target="MEDICAL PROVIDERS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d8">MEDICAL PROVIDERS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Medical providers are part of the patient's care team.</data>
      <data key="d12">MEDICAL PROVIDERS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CARE TEAM" target="TREATMENTS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENTS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The care team may prescribe medicines and determine which treatment is best for the patient.</data>
      <data key="d12">TREATMENTS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CARE TEAM" target="FAMILY MEMBERS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d8">FAMILY MEMBERS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The care team asks about the health history of family members who are blood relatives.</data>
      <data key="d12">FAMILY MEMBERS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CARE TEAM" target="BIOPSY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The care team orders and uses biopsy results as part of diagnosis and treatment</data>
      <data key="d12">BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CARE TEAM" target="CHEMOTHERAPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The care team helps manage side effects such as nausea and vomiting during chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d12">CARE TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CARE TEAM" target="PATIENT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The CARE TEAM and the PATIENT work together as partners in the patients healthcare journey. The care team is responsible for assembling and reviewing test results, using this information to manage and coordinate the patients ongoing care. In this collaborative relationship, the patient actively participates by discussing test results with the care team and following the recommendations provided. The patient is encouraged to communicate openly with the care team, share symptoms and concerns, and help the care team get to know them as an individual. In return, the care team provides care, guidance, and management based on the patients unique needs. Together, the CARE TEAM and the PATIENT form a cooperative unit focused on achieving the best possible health outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d12">CARE TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CARE TEAM" target="CANCER TREATMENT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER TREATMENT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The care team plans treatment and needs to know the stage and grade of the cancer</data>
      <data key="d12">CARE TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CARE TEAM" target="DOCTOR" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d8">DOCTOR</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The doctor is a member of the patient's care team. The care team includes the doctor as part of the patient's care team. Therefore, the doctor and the care team are connected: the doctor participates in the care team, which is responsible for the patient's care.</data>
      <data key="d12">DOCTOR</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CARE TEAM" target="CLINICAL TRIAL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d8">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The care team can be consulted to determine whether a patient qualifies for an available clinical trial.</data>
      <data key="d12">CARE TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CARE TEAM" target="BLOOD TESTS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d8">BLOOD TESTS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The care team is responsible for ordering and interpreting blood tests as part of the overall diagnosis and treatment process. In addition, the care team orders and reviews blood tests along with imaging results and reports, ensuring that all diagnostic information is evaluated together to guide patient care.</data>
      <data key="d12">CARE TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CARE TEAM" target="MRI" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d8">MRI</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Patients are advised to discuss any claustrophobia concerns with their care team prior to undergoing an MRI. This recommendation ensures that the care team can address anxiety or related issues before the procedure, helping to prepare the patient appropriately for the MRI experience.</data>
      <data key="d12">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CARE TEAM" target="TISSUE TESTS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d8">TISSUE TESTS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The care team orders and interprets tissue tests as part of diagnosis and treatment</data>
      <data key="d12">TISSUE TESTS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CARE TEAM" target="GENETIC TESTING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d8">GENETIC TESTING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The care team focuses on assessing cancer risk by inquiring about family history and the use of genetic testing. Specifically, they ask whether family members have undergone genetic testing, as this information helps clarify hereditary risk and guide patient care.</data>
      <data key="d12">GENETIC TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CARE TEAM" target="MEDICAL HISTORY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d8">MEDICAL HISTORY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The care team uses the patient's medical history to help determine which treatment is best. By reviewing the medical history, the care team can assess relevant health information and make informed decisions about the most appropriate course of treatment for the patient.</data>
      <data key="d12">MEDICAL HISTORY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CARE TEAM" target="FAMILY HISTORY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d8">FAMILY HISTORY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The care team asks about the health history of blood relatives (family history)</data>
      <data key="d12">FAMILY HISTORY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DOCTOR" target="HEALTH CARE PROVIDER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">DOCTOR</data>
      <data key="d8">HEALTH CARE PROVIDER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A doctor is a type of health care provider</data>
      <data key="d12">HEALTH CARE PROVIDER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DOCTOR" target="FAMILY MEMBERS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">DOCTOR</data>
      <data key="d8">FAMILY MEMBERS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The doctor asks about the health history of family members who are blood relatives</data>
      <data key="d12">FAMILY MEMBERS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DOCTOR" target="CARE TEAM" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">DOCTOR</data>
      <data key="d8">CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Doctor is a member of the patient's care team. As part of this team, the doctor works collaboratively with other care team members to plan and manage the patient's care.</data>
      <data key="d12">DOCTOR</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DOCTOR" target="GENETIC TEST" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">DOCTOR</data>
      <data key="d8">GENETIC TEST</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The doctor can talk to the patient about genetic testing.</data>
      <data key="d12">DOCTOR</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DOCTOR" target="GENETIC TESTING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">DOCTOR</data>
      <data key="d8">GENETIC TESTING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The doctor asks whether family members had genetic testing</data>
      <data key="d12">DOCTOR</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DOCTOR" target="DIAGNOSIS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">DOCTOR</data>
      <data key="d8">DIAGNOSIS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The doctor reviews the pathology report that contributes to the diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d12">DOCTOR</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DOCTOR" target="BIOPSY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">DOCTOR</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The doctor talks with the patient about whether a biopsy should be the next test.</data>
      <data key="d12">DOCTOR</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DOCTOR" target="SKIN BIOPSY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">DOCTOR</data>
      <data key="d8">SKIN BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A doctor performs a skin biopsy to diagnose squamous cell skin cancer and to inform risk assessment. The doctor selects the biopsy method based on the affected area's size, location, and other factors, and discusses the risk of scarring with the patient.</data>
      <data key="d12">DOCTOR</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DOCTOR" target="PATIENT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">DOCTOR</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Dr. [Doctor] provides ongoing care for the patient, including performing skin exams as part of their medical treatment. The patient, in turn, actively participates in their care by asking the doctor to review their pathology report. Together, these interactions reflect a collaborative doctor-patient relationship focused on evaluation, diagnosis, and informed treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d12">DOCTOR</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DOCTOR" target="IMAGING TESTS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">DOCTOR</data>
      <data key="d8">IMAGING TESTS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The doctor may order imaging tests during follow-up to check for cancer recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d12">DOCTOR</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DOCTOR" target="BLOOD TESTS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">DOCTOR</data>
      <data key="d8">BLOOD TESTS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The doctor discusses blood test results with the patient</data>
      <data key="d12">DOCTOR</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DOCTOR" target="CT SCAN" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">DOCTOR</data>
      <data key="d8">CT SCAN</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The doctor may recommend a CT scan if MRI is unavailable or not recommended.</data>
      <data key="d12">DOCTOR</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DOCTOR" target="PET SCAN" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">DOCTOR</data>
      <data key="d8">PET SCAN</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The doctor uses PET scan results to determine cancer extent or treatment response.</data>
      <data key="d12">DOCTOR</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DOCTOR" target="MRI SCAN" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">DOCTOR</data>
      <data key="d8">MRI SCAN</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The doctor reviews MRI results and discusses the next steps of care with the patient.</data>
      <data key="d12">DOCTOR</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="DOCTOR" target="GERMLINE TESTING" id="related_to">
