    <edge source="MEDICAL HISTORY" target="FAMILY HISTORY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MEDICAL HISTORY</data>
      <data key="d8">FAMILY HISTORY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Medical history and family history are two related but distinct health histories used by the care team. Family history is a specific component of the broader medical history. While the medical history encompasses a patients overall health background, the family history focuses specifically on the health conditions and patterns that occur among blood relatives. Both are important for guiding clinical care, but family history contributes to the medical history by providing insight into inherited risks and familial health trends.</data>
      <data key="d12">MEDICAL HISTORY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEDICAL HISTORY" target="CARE TEAM" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MEDICAL HISTORY</data>
      <data key="d8">CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The medical history is a record of a patients symptoms, medicines, and related health information, which is shared with the care team during consultations. The care team relies on this medical history to help determine the best treatment plan for the patient. In turn, the patient provides details about symptoms and current medications during the medical history process, enabling the care team to make informed decisions. Together, the medical history and the care team form a collaborative loop: the patient supplies essential information, and the care team uses it to guide treatment.</data>
      <data key="d12">MEDICAL HISTORY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEDICAL HISTORY" target="GENERAL HEALTH TESTS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MEDICAL HISTORY</data>
      <data key="d8">GENERAL HEALTH TESTS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">General health tests include taking a medical history</data>
      <data key="d12">MEDICAL HISTORY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEDICAL HISTORY" target="SURGERY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MEDICAL HISTORY</data>
      <data key="d8">SURGERY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Doctors ask about surgeries during medical history.</data>
      <data key="d12">MEDICAL HISTORY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MEDICAL HISTORY" target="BLOOD TRANSFUSION" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MEDICAL HISTORY</data>
      <data key="d8">BLOOD TRANSFUSION</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Doctors ask about blood transfusions during medical history.</data>
      <data key="d12">MEDICAL HISTORY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SYSTEMIC THERAPY" target="IMMUNOTHERAPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SYSTEMIC THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">IMMUNOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Immunotherapy is a type of systemic therapy, meaning it is administered throughout the body rather than targeting a single localized area. As a systemic therapy, immunotherapy works by leveraging the bodys immune system to fight cancer. Specifically, it increases immune activity to help identify and destroy cancer cells. Thus, within the broader category of systemic therapy, immunotherapy is distinguished by its mechanism of enhancing the immune response to combat malignancy.</data>
      <data key="d12">IMMUNOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PROGRESSION" target="IMMUNOTHERAPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PROGRESSION</data>
      <data key="d8">IMMUNOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">For patients whose cancer shows progression either during or after chemotherapy, immunotherapy is recommended as the next line of treatment for those who are eligible. This approach applies when the disease advances despite or following completion of chemotherapy, establishing immunotherapy as the standard subsequent therapeutic option.</data>
      <data key="d12">IMMUNOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PROGRESSIVE DISEASE" target="IMMUNOTHERAPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PROGRESSIVE DISEASE</data>
      <data key="d8">IMMUNOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Immunotherapy may be tried for locally progressive anal cancer before proceeding with surgery</data>
      <data key="d12">IMMUNOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NEUROLOGICAL EXAM" target="CARE TEAM" id="related_to">
