    <edge source="PATHOLOGIST" target="DOCTOR" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PATHOLOGIST</data>
      <data key="d8">DOCTOR</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A pathologist is a doctor who specializes in testing cells and tissue to diagnose disease. This specialist prepares a detailed pathology report based on those laboratory findings. Separately, the doctor who is caring for the patient reviews the pathology report prepared by the pathologist with the patient, explaining the results and their implications. Together, the pathologists diagnostic work and the doctors patient-facing review form a coordinated process: the pathologist identifies and interprets cellular and tissue abnormalities, while the doctor uses that information to guide the patients understanding and subsequent treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d12">DOCTOR</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATHOLOGIST" target="CARE TEAM" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PATHOLOGIST</data>
      <data key="d8">CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The care team is a multidisciplinary group of healthcare professionals who collaborate to provide comprehensive patient care. This team may include a pathologist, a physician who specializes in diagnosing disease by examining tissues, cells, and bodily fluids. In some contexts, the care team may include more than one pathologist, depending on the scope and complexity of care required. The pathologist plays a vital role within the care team by contributing diagnostic insights that guide treatment decisions. Thus, both the individual pathologist and the broader group of pathologists are recognized as potential members of the care team.</data>
      <data key="d12">PATHOLOGIST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATHOLOGIST" target="DIAGNOSIS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PATHOLOGIST</data>
      <data key="d8">DIAGNOSIS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The pathologist's examination informs the diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d12">PATHOLOGIST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATHOLOGIST" target="BIOPSY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PATHOLOGIST</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A pathologist examines the biopsy sample, studying the tissue and cells removed during a biopsy to determine the stage of a tumor. After this examination, the pathologist writes a pathology report detailing the findings.</data>
      <data key="d12">PATHOLOGIST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATHOLOGIST" target="SKIN BIOPSY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PATHOLOGIST</data>
      <data key="d8">SKIN BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A pathologist reviews a skin biopsy sample to determine whether squamous cell skin cancer is present. By analyzing the tissue sample obtained during the skin biopsy, the pathologist identifies the specific type and subtype of cancer, providing a precise diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d12">PATHOLOGIST</data>
    </edge>
